namespace pdg { /**
\mainpage %Pixel Dust Game Engine (PDG)

PDG is an open-source 2D game engine with JavaScript and C++ APIs. It provides
rendering, sprites, animation, input, audio, resources, events, and optional
physics. You write the application and game rules; PDG supplies the runtime
systems that run them.

This is the **JavaScript API reference** for the current source tree. Start with
<a href="topics.html">Topics</a> to browse by task, use the
<a href="annotated.html">class index</a> to find an object, or switch to the
<a href="../../cxx/html/index.html">C++ introduction</a>.

\section js_start Try the engine

The desktop `pdg` executable runs JavaScript using an embedded Node.js/V8 runtime.
From a configured and built source checkout, run these examples at the repository
root:

\code{.sh}
./test/demo spriter
./test/demo mvc
\endcode

The first is the animation showcase: ordinary and blended skeleton playback,
mouse aiming, a Drawing sword with spring recoil, and foot IK following a
rising, sliding rock. Let the opening zoom finish, move the pointer to a
character's facing side, and press **I** to toggle foot IK. The second is an
interactive gallery of MVC views, controls, dialogs, and drawing styles.

To run your own script, use `./pdg path/to/main.js`. Load the API with
`const pdg = require('pdg')` in the desktop runtime. Use ordinary Node.js modules
for services such as filesystem access, networking, and process management.
PDG supplies its own graphics, event dispatch, timers, and logging.

\section js_find Find the right API

| What you want to do | Where to start |
| --- | --- |
| Create a window and draw | GraphicsManager, Port, \ref Graphics |
| Style shapes, images, and text | Attributes and AnimatedAttributes; use Drawing for reusable artwork |
| Organize a scene | SpriteLayer, Sprite, TileLayer, \ref Sprites |
| Move objects or animate a character | Animated and \ref Animation |
| Respond to input and engine notifications | EventEmitter, EventManager, IEventHandler, \ref Events |
| Load resources, schedule work, or play audio | \ref Managers and \ref Sound |
| Save or transmit structured state | Serializer, Deserializer, \ref Serialization |
| Add physics or PDG networking helpers | \ref Physics and \ref Network |

\section js_model Drawing, sprites, and events

A Port is a drawing surface. Respond to `pdg.eventType_PortDraw` to issue custom
drawing commands through its port. Attributes carries colors, gradients,
strokes, text and image settings, and transforms. Drawing stores reusable
elements, and ElementRef provides explicit editing access. Drawing elements
currently copy their Attributes; changing the original Attributes afterward
does not update those elements.

For a managed scene, put Sprites in a SpriteLayer. Its transform and ordering
apply to its sprites, and the engine handles their normal animation and drawing.
Animated provides programmed movement, rotation, and size changes. For example,
with an existing sprite in an active layer:

\code{.js}
sprite.moveTo(new pdg.Point(240, 180), 0.5, pdg.easeInOutQuad);
sprite.rotateBy(Math.PI / 2, 0.5, pdg.easeInOutQuad,
              pdg.rotationDirection_Clockwise);
\endcode

This moves the sprite and turns it clockwise over half a second. You schedule
the changes once; do not also call `animate()` each frame on an object that the
engine already advances.

PDG events use EventEmitter and EventManager, separately from Node.js's event
system. Sprite events propagate from the Sprite to its SpriteLayer and then to
the EventManager until handled. See \ref Events for event constants and payloads.

The optional MVC framework in `src/js/mvc-app/` adds controllers, views, dialogs,
and controls. The framework README and control gallery show its module imports,
layout and input handling, and application lifecycle.

\section js_animation Character animation

Sprite supports image-frame playback and Spriter SCML animation. Its pose APIs
add clip sampling and seeking, blends and transitions, bone/binding transforms,
procedural modifiers, two-bone IK, spring targets, contact locks, and custom
Drawing artwork attached to a pose. Optional Chipmunk integration supports
physical animation rigs and recovery to authored animation.

Start with Sprite::enableAnimationPose() and check its result before using the
pose-based extensions: their rig contract is narrower than ordinary Spriter
playback. Sprite::addAnimationIK() registers a two-bone constraint;
Sprite::setAnimationIKTarget() updates its target. See
\ref animation_target_adapters for spring/contact helpers and
\ref animation_trigger_event for authored trigger notifications.

Part currently provides an independently animated transform, identity,
hierarchy, and optional bone binding under a Sprite. Create it with
`sprite.createPart(name)`, rather than `new pdg.Part()`. `getBoneId()` safely
returns `pdg.boneId_None` when unbound. Sprite and Part expose an read-only
`.physics` property that returns `pdg.PhysicsBody.NoPhysics` until setupPhysicsBody() is
called. Physical velocity, damping, forces and impulses belong to PhysicsBody;
Animated supplies programmed movement/spin and sizing. Sprite bodies use Chipmunk
on enabled layers or PDG's basic integrator otherwise. Part bodies currently use
the basic integrator; Part artwork and contacts/constraints are still in development.
AnimatedAttributes combines programmed transforms with eased appearance channels,
without physics or automatic live updates to Drawing elements. The new body bindings are available in V8 and
JavaScriptCore; browser/Wasm body bindings remain pending.

\section js_conventions Timing and API conventions

- Animation durations and elapsed steps use **floating-point seconds**: `0.25`
  means a quarter second. This includes Animated motion, sprite fades, layer
  fades/zoom, animation helpers, and easing callbacks.
- Angles are radians. Use integer constants such as
  `pdg.rotationDirection_Clockwise` and `pdg.animationSpace_World` for modes and
  coordinate spaces. Asset, clip, bone, and part names are strings.
- Frame numbers are integers, frame-playback rates are frames per second, and
  blend weights are dimensionless.
- TimerManager delays and event timestamps retain their documented millisecond
  units. Convert an elapsed millisecond interval with `/ 1000` before passing
  it to `animate(deltaSeconds)`. For authored triggers, use `timeSeconds` and
  `offsetSeconds`; `frameTime` is a compatibility millisecond field.
- Some objects are created through factories. Follow the class's construction
  instructions. Copy a borrowed pose view if you need it after its callback;
  a JavaScript reference alone does not extend that view's validity.

\section js_builds Runtime and build capabilities

The source tree includes desktop builds for macOS, Windows, and Linux, headless
builds and a Node.js addon, an iOS/JavaScriptCore integration, and an
Emscripten/WebAssembly browser build. The browser target uses compiled engine
code, and does not supply desktop Node.js services. API coverage varies between
targets, particularly for newly added interfaces.

Headless builds omit windowing and graphics; audio depends on the target.
Spriter animation and Chipmunk physics are optional build capabilities. Query
Sprite::supportsAnimationPhysics() and Sprite::isAnimationDrawingSupported()
where applicable. Seeing an API in this reference does not enable an omitted
runtime feature.

Source builds use `./configure` and `make` on POSIX, or `configure.ps1` and
`make.ps1` on Windows; initialize Git submodules when obtaining the source.
Script regressions live in `test/spec/`, client entry points in `test/js/`, and
animation demo assets and instructions in `test/data/animation-demo/`.
Run specs with `./test/unit` (embedded runtime), `./test/unit --node` (Node addon),
`./test/unit --web` (browser), or `./test/unit --ios` (Simulator).
`./test/rigs` runs rig integration checks under Node; `./test/ui` opens visual
pages, `./test/tools` validates test/build tooling, and `./test/perf` runs
benchmarks. See `test/README.md` in the source checkout for requirements,
selection, automated runs, and artifacts.

\section api_stability API stability

Class notes describe compatibility commitments, independently of implementation
quality and platform support. The same public API has the same level in both
language references; language-specific wrappers may have their own rating.

| Level | Commitment |
| --- | --- |
| 1 — Experimental | Signatures, behavior or availability may change without a compatibility period. |
| 2 — Evolving | Implemented and supported; the contract is still settling. Compatibility is preserved where practical and breaking changes are documented in release notes. |
| 3 — Stable | Established public contract. Intentional breaking changes require an announced breaking release and migration guidance. |

Methods, options, event payloads and result structures inherit their owning API's
level unless separately marked. A stable Serializer/Deserializer interface does
not guarantee stable snapshot bytes: supported record versions, resource policies
and restoration restrictions are documented separately. Build requirements remain
in force at every level; animation physics requires Chipmunk.

The application framework's views, controls, application classes and appearance
classes are **Level 2 — Evolving** in both C++ and JavaScript. Its JavaScript guide
is `src/js/mvc-app/README.md`; C++ declarations live under `pdg/app/` and are not
included in this engine reference. Unrated utilities and internal implementation
types carry no new compatibility promise from this policy.

\section js_about About PDG

PDG began in 2003 with Ed Zavada at Pixel Dust Games for Catan Online, continued
at Dream Rock Studios, and was released under the MIT license in 2012. Its
focus remains reusable 2D engine and application systems. Game-specific AI,
pathfinding, rules, and content-authoring workflows belong to the application.

\section js_platform_requirements Platform requirements

The native engine requires a C++20 compiler and standard library, including
`std::format`. The iOS application requires **iOS 16.3 or later**.

*/ }
